Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis (PrEP) Initiation / Urgent Refill
General Practice Other
High-risk sexual behaviour, Request for HIV prevention, Negative baseline HIV test, Normal renal function

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-031
Reason for Visit: Extreme anxiety and urgent request for prophylactic HIV medication following the immediate depletion of current stock prior to engaging in imminent high-risk behaviour tonight.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who realized they had entirely depleted their supply of daily PrEP. The patient is scheduled to attend a multi-day event tonight involving unavoidable, ongoing high-risk sexual behaviour, specifically condomless receptive anal intercourse. Urgent face-to-face assessment was required to immediately prescribe a bridging supply to maintain continuous pharmacological protection, as missing doses immediately preceding high-risk exposure severely compromises efficacy. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period would have exposed the patient to an unacceptable and preventable risk of HIV acquisition tonight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I just ran out of my PrEP pills, I'm heading out to a major event in two hours, and I absolutely cannot risk catching HIV tonight."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a history of ongoing high-risk sexual behaviour and a specific request for an urgent refill of prophylactic medication to prevent HIV acquisition. The patient has been strictly compliant with daily Tenofovir/Emtricitabine but failed to secure a timely refill. The patient confirms an absolute absence of symptoms indicating an acute retroviral syndrome (no fever, myalgia, or rash). A review of recent pathology confirms an established negative HIV status and an evaluation of renal function prior to prescribing tenofovir-based regimens shows a normal eGFR 4 weeks ago. Comprehensive screening for co-occurring sexually transmitted infections was also recently completed and negative. The patient is highly distressed about the impending lapse in coverage.
Functional Status: Fully independent and asymptomatic.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of infectious disease transmission if pharmaceutical prophylaxis lapses.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, visibly anxious regarding the immediate lack of medication.
Physical Exam: General: No lymphadenopathy, no pharyngeal erythema, no maculopapular rash, confirming clinical absence of acute seroconversion illness. The clinical presentation is aligned with a request for HIV prevention, a recently documented negative baseline HIV test, and normal renal function. The encounter required counselling regarding strict daily adherence to ensure efficacy and a reiteration of the necessity for regular 3-monthly follow-up testing. Expected investigation results include a formal, updated negative fourth-generation HIV antigen/antibody test and a normal serum creatinine/eGFR enabling the safe ongoing prescription of the antiretroviral medication.
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe home environment; patient is actively preparing to leave for the event.
Medication Reconciliation: Tenofovir disoproxil/Emtricitabine 300/200mg daily; physically verified the empty blister pack.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis (PrEP) Urgent Refill - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute HIV Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of acute retroviral syndrome symptoms and continuous prior adherence to the prophylactic regimen up until today.

Renal Impairment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: A recent, documented normal eGFR from the regular prescribing physician 4 weeks ago.

Other Acute STIs - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of dysuria, purulent discharge, or genital ulceration on history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Infectious Risk)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient faced an immediate, critical lapse in HIV prophylaxis directly preceding planned, high-risk sexual exposure. Telephone triage accurately identified the time-sensitive nature of maintaining steady-state drug levels for PrEP efficacy. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to verify the patient's clinical stability, confirm the absence of acute HIV symptoms, and immediately issue a bridging prescription. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in the patient engaging in high-risk behaviour entirely unprotected, creating a massive, preventable public health risk of HIV transmission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a rapid clinical assessment to exclude acute seroconversion illness. Prescribed a 30-day bridging supply of Tenofovir disoproxil/Emtricitabine 300/200mg. Reinforced the absolute necessity of taking the medication daily.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent future catastrophic lapses in your medication, permanently set a recurring calendar alert on your phone for exactly 14 days before your pills run out. Label it "Book PrEP Blood Tests." By linking the blood test requirement to a firm two-week buffer, you actively force yourself into the medical system with enough time to secure the results and the subsequent script without reaching a crisis point.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Must attend regular GP or Sexual Health Clinic within 30 days for formal 3-monthly STI screening and renal function panel.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to a medical professional immediately if you develop a severe flu-like illness, a widespread rash, or swollen lymph nodes in the weeks following the event, as this may indicate a failure of prophylaxis.

